Richard Bair: I think that's always going to be our normal stay. So, we'll have, like, I was talking to Ty yesterday about Paymaster HIP, again, and we've, we've got some presentation we're going to do today for some HIP, and obviously there's

75
00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:15.560
Richard Bair: all kinds of new stuff for block nodes, and all sorts of things that we'll be doing over the course of the year. So I kind of bucket all those into sort of the continuous improvement, continuous feature development sort of phase, you know, bucket, if you will.

76
00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:28.709
Richard Bair: The second thing that I'm really, really, passionate about that, I want to put a lot of effort into this year is sort of continuous improvement on the performance,

77
00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:39.690
Richard Bair: on the performance level. And there's a bunch of things that we've talked about, for a while that we're actually ready to execute on. One of those

78
00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:46.159
Richard Bair: is, just putting real focus and emphasis on our latencies. Right now.

79
00:09:46.470 --> 00:10:05.740
Richard Bair: We have something along the lines of, 3 seconds, I think, is our end-to-end latency. Oh, okay. It's more like 5 seconds end-to-end, including MirrorNode, and it's like 3 seconds for, from the time that you submit a transaction to when the transaction reaches consensus across the network.

80
00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:19.760
Richard Bair: somewhere around 3 seconds. And… which is well within the SLAs that we've always lived by, but we can do a lot better than that. And we've started to do measurements on, latencies

81
00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:29.380
Richard Bair: Throughout the system. I mean, we have metrics that we've built in from, you know, for years and years. From before I got here, there was metrics to keep track of

82
00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:48.179
Richard Bair: you know, how long it takes to reach consensus on a transaction. But we are… we've added more to the performance harness, where, for example, when you submit a transaction, it now measures it all the way through the point where a block node would receive that block, so we can… we can get, you know, that measurement.

83
00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:59.850
Richard Bair: And, you know, there's a lot of use cases, especially on spheres, where people want to have lower latencies in their sphere. And on the globally distributed network.

84
00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:19.040
Richard Bair: I think, you know, the main limitation that we have is going to be the speed of light, because we have these nodes globally distributed for, you know, maximal decentralization purposes, and obviously, whatever transaction you have has to be gossiped to all these different nodes.

85
00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:34.470
Richard Bair: And the most efficient gossip algorithms and everything can't overcome, obviously, the basic limitations of the physics. So, I think, generally, for a globally distributed network, the best that you can get is probably somewhere around, you know, 300, 400 milliseconds.

86
00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:52.099
Richard Bair: Which would be one order of magnitude faster than what we do today. So, the sort of sales pitch that I give to the team is, I want to see our latencies improve by an order of magnitude. So, if we could go from, you know, 3 seconds down to around, you know, 300, 400 milliseconds, then,

87
00:11:52.100 --> 00:12:01.020
Richard Bair: it's definitely achievable. It's also not trivial, so it's a… it's a good lofty goal to try to strive for.

88
00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:15.369
Richard Bair: And I think that on an internal network, it should be the case that if you brought, like, say, in a sphere, let's say that you had all of your machines in the same, country, or something like that, you should be able to see your latencies come down, you know.

89
00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:20.440
Richard Bair: what's the right word? Correspondingly.

90
00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:27.670
Richard Bair: So I think, reducing latency is one of those things that's important to me. Another one is that,

91
00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:34.479
Richard Bair: we actually get really much higher than 10K TPS on various tests that we run.

92
00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:42.090
Richard Bair: But not on all tests. There's some tests that do really well, and, you know, when you look at our system.

93
00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:54.949
Richard Bair: We have a lot of flexibility and power in all the different types of transactions that we can execute. And if you look at, like, an Ethereum transaction, it's totally up to, you know, how much gas is being executed by the guy. So, TPS is not, like, a,

94
00:12:54.970 --> 00:13:04.410
Richard Bair: an even metric for all different transaction types, or for different configurations of transactions. Every different type of transaction has its own configuration.

95
00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:24.769
Richard Bair: So what we do today is we run a whole bunch of performance tests, and we say, oh, okay, these are, safe limits. You know, here's, like, the limits that we're seeing, and let's add a huge safety factor in there so that we know that on mainnet, if somebody runs any type of transaction, it's not going to max out our resources, because we don't want

96
00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:33.730
Richard Bair: that to happen. If that were to happen, then somebody could, you know, basically do denial of service on the network. We don't like, you know, that doesn't sound like a great thing, so we don't want to be able to do that.

97
00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:45.619
Richard Bair: So we have this huge safety buffer that we have in all of our performance. But, the reason that we do that is because, we don't have a way to sort of dynamically adjust

98
00:13:45.780 --> 00:14:02.039
Richard Bair: the throttling on the system based on the actual load that we're under. So, for example, if all that was happening on the network were, say, crypto transfers or consensus submit messages.

99
00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:08.420
Richard Bair: you know… Maybe we could do 30,000 transactions per second. Maybe we could do 50,000 transactions per second.

100
00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:25.729
Richard Bair: But we don't have, you know, but then if somebody were to take that crypto transfer, and instead of doing a basic crypto transfer, they decided that they were going to do some that included custom fees and had, you know, 10 different parties involved in the transfer, now maybe you can only do, you know, 10,000 of those per second.

101
00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:32.389
Richard Bair: So, in the absence of a dynamic throttle, we just have to throttle that worst-case scenario.

102
00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:47.570
Richard Bair: And that means that we don't get the maximal possible utility out of the network. So, another one of the things that we want to work on, and I don't, personally, I don't know that this could be completed through the year, because it's still in research mode.

103
00:14:47.570 --> 00:15:00.529
Richard Bair: But it's, what we call our dynamic throttles, and it would be the ability to have the system dynamically figure out what the health of the network is, and adjust throttles correspondingly.

104
00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:09.819
Richard Bair: And what makes this even more interesting is, if you were in a sort of mixed node environment where you have

105
00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:29.449
Richard Bair: you know, some fast nodes and some slow nodes. Like, suppose anybody could run a node. In that case, you don't really have any guarantee of what kind of computer people are running. And, you know, there's a pretty good economic incentive for people to run the cheapest, barest node that they can get away with.

106
00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:48.180
Richard Bair: Because then they get the maximum, you know, return on running their node. So, you would imagine that in an anonymous node world where anybody could run any kind of computer they wanted to on the network, you would see a lot of Raspberry Pis right now, for example, because the load on the network is not that high, so a bunch of people would just run the cheapest thing they could.

107
00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:53.029
Richard Bair: Which, by the way, we've measured the Raspberry Pi at around 1000 TPS, if anybody's interested.

108
00:15:53.030 --> 00:16:13.559
Richard Bair: So, it's like, you know, you really could. You could just run a little Raspberry Pi on the network, and meanwhile, you know, the honest people, if you will, are out there running these big, massive servers, and then some people are running little pies. What we want with a dynamic throttle system, we want to make it so that the whole network sort of auto-levels based on its actual capabilities.

109
00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:15.129
Richard Bair: Whether that's,

110
00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:29.789
Richard Bair: Yeah, by the way, Milan, if you want to run the Raspberry Pi at that speed, the only thing you need is a really good disk attached to it. So, if you get a good SSD and attach it to the Pi, it'll run happily. You can use Solo to run a single-node network on their benchmark it.

111
00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:38.030
Richard Bair: but, yeah, so it really comes down to the…

112
00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:41.910
Richard Bair: Desire to be able to create a system

113
00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:59.839
Richard Bair: that would be able to stay happy and healthy, and it would auto-level itself from a performance perspective based on the capability of the network itself, and the type of transactions that are being executed. And we wouldn't have to have sort of a fixed throttle system. And I know that, like, in… in…

114
00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:04.120
Richard Bair: on Twitter, this has been something people have talked about forever, they've always…

115
00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:20.009
Richard Bair: they never talked about dynamic throttles, of course, but they'd always talk about, oh, well, we just have the network, you know, artificially limited, it could actually go much faster. And so, our thought was, if we could implement these dynamic throttles, then

116
00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:23.919
Richard Bair: it actually accomplishes two goals. The first goal is, un…

117
00:17:24.130 --> 00:17:28.079
Richard Bair: Uncapping the network so that it runs at its maximal.

118
00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:30.639
Richard Bair: Capability, and the second one is

119
00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:36.540
Richard Bair: It also protects the network in the case of, like, really atrocious… excuse me…

120
00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:44.250
Richard Bair: Types of transactions that would… or hardware mixtures that would otherwise be bad for the network, so…

121
00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:49.149
Richard Bair: Anyway, on the performance angle, those are my main things, is, like, latency and the throttle system.

122
00:17:49.350 --> 00:18:04.169
Richard Bair: And then there's a lot of, I, you know, just sort of technical debt work that I want to work on throughout the year. One of the things is, we put a lot of effort, a couple, you know, a year, year and a half ago in delivering modularity.

123
00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:08.449
Richard Bair: And, we sort of need to take another pass,

124
00:18:08.710 --> 00:18:13.240
Richard Bair: There's a, you know, it's, it's an,

125
00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:17.979
Richard Bair: A very well-known, very hard thing to do.

126
00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:29.690
Richard Bair: is to essentially rewrite your system, without blowing up your system. You know, it's a… it's a well-documented phenomenon. Most people who attempt it fail.

127
00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:47.390
Richard Bair: Because what happens is that when you would go to do a rewrite, a lot of that, you know, crufty code that you threw away was actually necessary, because there are all these edge cases and scenarios that weren't properly documented, that nobody knew about, and you just threw that away during the rewrite, or you,

128
00:18:47.510 --> 00:19:00.109
Richard Bair: You run into problems where, you know, you try to gold plate the second version, you know, it's called the second system effect. Try to gold plate the second implementation, and it just takes forever to complete, and so forth.

129
00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:12.860
Richard Bair: So what the team did when we did modularity was… it was actually an incredibly hard thing to pull off, and they did it flawlessly. I mean, we… we rewrote that system on the fly, and nobody

130
00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:27.599
Richard Bair: was the wiser. We, were able to deliver that in production without, you know, a whole bunch of new bugs and so forth. It was really an incredible job. But, there was a moment in time when we…

131
00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:36.079
Richard Bair: During that process, where, you know, initially, we're doing the modularity, and it's a very clean design, and we're like, oh yeah, we've got this figured out.

132
00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:52.960
Richard Bair: And then… and then, you know, you get towards the latter part of such implementations, and you'd start to discover all the wrinkles and details that you didn't know about when you started. And we were at that point in the process where it's like, you know what? Just commit some crimes, write some code, get it out of here.

133
00:19:52.960 --> 00:20:02.079
Richard Bair: And we'll come back and clean it up later, because if we stop and try to redo everything at this point, we'll have to add another year to this effort, and we just can't afford it. So…

134
00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:17.779
Richard Bair: we… we were able to deliver, modularity, but I would say it's probably, like, 70% of what I would want. You know, there's… there's still, like, 30% of craft hanging around that we need to figure out. And a lot of that comes into the, sort of, the basic,

135
00:20:18.340 --> 00:20:35.980
Richard Bair: systems and services support that are in there for things like, you know, computing fees or whatever. So, we have a hip going on for cleaning up our fees system, and we never touched the throttle system, in part because we were waiting to see if we could figure out dynamic throttles.

136
00:20:35.980 --> 00:20:46.290
Richard Bair: And then we could touch that. But there's a lot of other things, too. Like, if you wanted to, do sort of a custom build of a high-row network, like, let's say you wanted to do a sphere.

137
00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:55.599
Richard Bair: and you wanted to add some new service that didn't exist before. There's, like, I don't know, maybe 20 or 30 places that you have to go touch.

138
00:20:55.750 --> 00:21:14.430
Richard Bair: And it's not really clean, in that you have to, like, fork the code, and then you have to go touch those things. And then as we update the code upstream, you have to, like, update your fork, and rebuild and adjust to changes, and it's really just kind of a laborious, nasty process.

139
00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:32.089
Richard Bair: So, what we really want to do is make it so that we can, have a really nice, model for service developers. So, if you wanted to have a hiero network, and then add your own custom service to the system, you should be able to do it without having to fork the system. You should be able to just

140
00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:37.640
Richard Bair: Take our existing modules, Drop in your own module, and have it run.

141
00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:44.489
Richard Bair: And that is not something that we can do today, and so that's something that I'm looking at also for next year, I think would be,

142
00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:53.610
Richard Bair: Or for this year, I should say. Just a sort of more cleanup on the services level that would make it easier to do this sort of a thing.

143
